# Workflow Database Design

Version: 1.0
Status: Approved
Document Owner: Project Team
Last Updated: July 2026

---

# 1. Purpose

This document defines the Firestore database architecture for the Workflow MVP.

The database is designed to support a modern SaaS application while remaining simple, scalable, secure, and optimized for Firebase's free (Spark) plan.

The primary design goals are:

- Fast data retrieval
- Low Firestore read count
- Simple relationships
- Easy maintenance
- Tenant isolation
- Explainable assignment workflow
- Enterprise-style data organization

---

# 2. Database Design Principles

The following principles guide every database decision.

## 2.1 Read Optimization First

Firestore charges primarily based on document reads.

The database should minimize reads instead of minimizing storage.

Small amounts of duplicated data are acceptable if they significantly reduce database queries.

---

## 2.2 One Screen = Few Reads

Every major screen should require as few Firestore reads as possible.

Target:

| Screen | Target Reads |
|----------|-------------|
| Dashboard | 2–4 |
| Task List | 1 |
| Task Details | 1–2 |
| Employee List | 1 |
| Employee Details | 1 |
| Settings | 1 |

---

## 2.3 Embed Small Data

Small data that always belongs to another entity should be embedded instead of creating separate collections.

Examples:

- Assignment
- Proof
- Timeline
- Recommendation summary

This reduces Firestore reads.

---

## 2.4 Separate Independent Data

Large or independently managed entities should have their own collection.

Examples:

- Users
- Organizations
- Recommendations
- Incidents
- Notifications
- Audit Logs

---

## 2.5 Soft Delete

Business records should never be permanently deleted.

Instead:

```text
isActive = false
```

Benefits:

- Better auditing
- Easier recovery
- No broken references
- Enterprise-friendly design

---

## 2.6 Tenant Isolation

Every business document belongs to exactly one organization.

Every document must contain:

```text
organizationId
```

Firestore Security Rules must enforce organization isolation.

---

## 2.7 Role-Based Security

Access is controlled using:

- Authentication
- User role
- organizationId

Frontend validation improves user experience.

Firestore Security Rules provide actual security.

---

# 3. Database Overview

Workflow uses Cloud Firestore.

The MVP consists of only seven top-level collections.

```text
organizations
users
tasks
recommendations
incidents
notifications
auditLogs
```

This structure keeps the database simple while supporting all required project features.

