# 5. Embedded Data

The following objects are embedded inside the Task document.

## Assignment

Contains:

- Assigned employee
- Assignment status
- Assignment timestamp

---

## Proof

Contains:

- Uploaded file
- Verification status
- Verification information

---

## Timeline

Contains:

- Major task events
- Timestamp
- User responsible

---

## Recommendation Summary

Contains:

- Top recommended employee
- Recommendation score
- Current recommendation status

---

## Incident Summary

Contains:

- Whether incidents exist
- Number of open incidents

Only summary information is stored inside the task.

Detailed incident records remain inside the Incidents collection.

---

# 6. Why Embedded Objects?

Embedding reduces Firestore reads.

Instead of:

```text
Task

↓

Assignment

↓

Proof

↓

Timeline
```

Workflow loads:

```text
Task

↓

Everything needed
```

This significantly improves page loading speed.

---

# 7. Firestore Collections

| Collection | Purpose |
|------------|----------|
| organizations | Organization information |
| users | Admins, managers and employees |
| tasks | Operational work items |
| recommendations | AI recommendation history |
| incidents | Operational issues |
| notifications | User notifications |
| auditLogs | System audit history |

---

# 8. Why No Employee Collection?

Employees are users.

The Users collection stores:

- Admin
- Manager
- Employee

The role determines system behavior.

This avoids duplicate data and simplifies authentication.

---

# 9. Why No Assignment Collection?

Each task can only have one active assignment.

Therefore assignment information is stored directly inside the Task document.

Benefits:

- One less collection
- Fewer reads
- Simpler queries

---

# 10. Why No Proof Collection?

Work proof belongs only to its task.

Embedding proof information inside the task avoids unnecessary document lookups.

The uploaded file itself remains in Firebase Storage.

---

# 11. Document Ownership

Every business document must contain:

- organizationId
- createdAt
- updatedAt
- isActive

Where applicable it should also contain:

- createdBy
- updatedBy

This ensures traceability across the system.

---

# 12. Performance Strategy

The database is optimized for:

- Minimal document reads
- Small query sizes
- Lightweight dashboard loading
- Embedded task information
- Organization-level filtering

Large joins should be avoided.

---

# 13. Firebase Spark Plan Compatibility

The architecture is intentionally designed to remain within Firebase's free tier during MVP development.

Key optimizations include:

- Minimal number of collections
- Embedded objects where appropriate
- No unnecessary subcollections
- No Cloud Functions
- No custom backend
- Efficient Firestore queries
- Small document sizes
- Reduced document reads

---

# 14. Database Rules

The following rules apply throughout the project.

- Every business document must include organizationId.
- Every collection must support soft deletion using isActive.
- Firestore reads should be minimized.
- Duplicate small values when it improves performance.
- Keep documents focused and reasonably sized.
- Store files in Firebase Storage, not Firestore.
- Do not create unnecessary collections.

---

# 15. Acceptance Criteria

The database design is considered complete when:

- Only approved collections exist.
- Tenant isolation is supported.
- Read optimization principles are followed.
- Embedded objects reduce unnecessary queries.
- Firestore Spark plan limits are respected.
- Soft deletion is implemented.
- The structure supports all MVP features without unnecessary complexity.
